  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={5747-5755},
  abstract={Diabetes is a chronic disease with a high prevalence globally, and the rapidly growing body of related literature provides a rich big data resource for answering diabetes-related questions. However, traditional literature mining or question answering still faces many problems, such as low processing efficiency, difficulty in integrating heterogeneous knowledge, and the lack of precision and traceability in retrieval results. Although Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown significant potential in the medical question answering domain, their performance is limited by insufficient integration of complex structured information from the literature, leading to deficiencies in accuracy, explainability, and the effective utilization of structured knowledge. To address this, we propose a Triple-Fusion Retrieval-Augmented Generation framework with a diabetes Knowledge Graph (DTRAG). The core contributions of this framework are twofold: First, we construct a high-quality diabetes knowledge graph and resolve the issue of fragmented relation paths caused by inconsistent entity naming through a novel entity alias mapping mechanism. Second, we design a triple-fusion retrieval method that synergistically merges three types of information-path retrieval, neighbor entity retrieval, and entity explanation retrieval-and inputs the fused context into an LLM for unified reasoning and generation. This approach enhances the accuracy and explainability of the answers. Extensive experimental results demonstrate that DTRAG outperforms both general and biomedical domain-specific LLMs on diabetes question answering tasks, showcasing superior domain-specific question answering capabilities and more effective knowledge utilization.},
